=== (9) ASSUMPTIONS THIS SPEC MAKES — flag back rather than guessing if any is wrong ===
(i) `.claude-plugin/plugin.json` is in files_in_scope for the version bump even though the issue body's Files list names only two files: the every-functional-commit bump convention requires it, and B1's shipped `scope_compliance` gate check would flag an edit to a file not named here.
(ii) The refusal is a THROWN tagged error mapped by `main`, not a `{code:1}` return from dispatchCmd — the issue specifies the exit code and JSON shape but not the mechanism. Rationale: every other refusal in dispatchCmd throws, and a returned code-1 object would be a shape existing callers (which read `res.json.dispatch_id`) could misread — a gate must fail closed by construction.
(iii) `warnings` entries carry an added `severity: 'fail' | 'warn'` field alongside spec-lint's own `{check, detail}`. The issue says only "as warnings[]"; the severity makes a DEMOTED heuristic failure distinguishable from an ordinary warning, which is what keeps the demotion auditable rather than silent. This is a dispatch-JSON-local shape; it is NOT a change to spec-lint's Diagnostic.
(iv) `lintSpec` runs ONLY for executor-profile dispatches, so a non-executor dispatch has no `warnings` key at all and no lintSpec code path — the strongest structural form of the never-widen invariant.
(v) The issue's test item "a judgment-role dispatch with no `--spec` is unaffected" is vacuous as literally written: dispatch.mjs:1248 already rejects a missing `--spec` for EVERY role, before any of this. The invariant it was reaching for is covered by the schema-invalid-spec-on-backend-lead test instead; the literal case is kept as a cheap one-line regression, explicitly commented as pre-existing behaviour.

